{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 7, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Poitiers and played a significant role in the history of the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. \n*Early Life* \nRaymond III was born around 1140 and was the heir to the County of Tripoli. His father Raymond II was killed in 1152 during an attack by the Nizari Ismailis also known as the Assassins. \n*Succession and Reign* \nAfter the death of his father Raymond III succeeded him as the Count of Tripoli. He was still a minor at the time and the regency was held by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. \n*Marriage and Alliances* \nRaymond III married Hodierna of Jerusalem's niece also named Hodierna. This marriage helped to strengthen the alliance between the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. Raymond III also formed an alliance with the Templars and the Hospitallers. \nRaymond III was known for his military campaigns against the Muslim states in Syria. He also played a significant role in the Battle of Inab in 1149. \n*Capture and Imprisonment* \nRaymond III was captured by the Nizari Ismailis in 1164 and was imprisoned for several years. He was released in 1173 after a ransom was paid. \n*Later Life and Death* \nAfter his release Raymond III returned to Tripoli and continued to rule the county. He died in 1187 without an heir. The County of Tripoli was then inherited by his cousin Bohemond IV of Antioch. \n*Legacy* \nRaymond III played a significant role in the history of the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was known for his military campaigns and his alliances with the Templars and the Hospitallers. His legacy continued to shape the history of the region for centuries to come.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"tok